  "live_records": {
    "q-9534": {
      "dom_id": "step-q9534",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=2{e}^{3\\theta }$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{6\\theta }+36{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{40{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
