    "q-15123": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15123",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{({\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-15123-a",
