    "q-15133": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15133",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14183798/assets/3a760f55b6437a39010e4d1a00bdb5fb5390d848dd74347e7736e2ac49cd562b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?",
      "worked_solution": "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}+(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+144{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-15133-a",
              "type": "math",
